const BISMARCK = L("s9i", "Bismarck", [0, 44, 25, 56], ["berlin", "koniggratz", "sedan", "vienna"], [
  S("Otto von Bismarck became Prussia's minister-president in September 1862. Three wars followed: Denmark (1864), Austria (1866, decided at Königgrätz on 3 July) and France (1870 to 1871, with Sedan on 1 September 1870). The German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles on 18 January 1871.", { w: 1, ...X }),
  S("As chancellor until 1890 he worked to keep France isolated. He feared a coalition against Germany, in the middle of Europe. His answer was a web of treaties: the Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary (1879), the Triple Alliance with Italy added (1882), and the Reinsurance Treaty with Russia (1887).", { w: 1, ...X }),
  Q("Which was NOT a constraint on Bismarck?", "A parliament that chose the chancellor by majority vote", ["An emperor who could dismiss him at will", "A Reichstag that voted the army's budget", "France, which wanted back Alsace and Lorraine"], "The chancellor answered to the emperor, not the Reichstag. He had to find majorities for the budget, but could not be voted out of office.", { k: "constraint", w: 1 }),
  Q("Which was luck, not Bismarck's design?", "The death of Frederick III in 1888, after 99 days as emperor", ["The Reinsurance Treaty signed with Russia in 1887", "The Dual Alliance concluded with Austria in 1879", "The Berlin Congress held in 1878 under his chair"], "A liberal emperor's short reign put Wilhelm II on the throne in June 1888, and Wilhelm dismissed Bismarck in March 1890. The treaties and the congress were choices.", { k: "luck", w: 1, ...X }),
  Q("Which statement about the Ems dispatch of July 1870 is defensible?", "Bismarck edited it to sound sharper; historians argue how far he sought war", ["It was a treaty by which France declared war", "It was entirely invented by the French foreign minister", "It is agreed that Bismarck was surprised by the outcome"], "He shortened a telegram on the king's talk with the French ambassador so it read as a snub. The war, historians say, was wanted on both sides, to different degrees.", { k: "reading", w: 1, ...X }),
  O("Put these in order, earliest first.", ["The Austro-Prussian War ends at Königgrätz", "The German Empire is proclaimed at Versailles", "The Dual Alliance with Austria-Hungary", "The Reinsurance Treaty with Russia"], "1866, 1871, 1879 and 1887.", { w: 1, ...X }),
  Q("After Bismarck's dismissal, what happened to the Reinsurance Treaty and its effect?", "Germany let it lapse; Russia and France drew together", ["It was renewed by Wilhelm II and strengthened", "Russia and Austria joined the Triple Alliance", "Britain took Germany's place in the treaty"], "Wilhelm's advisers did not renew it. A French-Russian military convention followed in 1892 (ratified in 1894).", { w: 1, ...X }),
  T("Tap Berlin, the seat of the chancellor whose diplomacy the Congress of 1878 made famous.", "berlin", ["vienna", "sedan", "koniggratz"], "Berlin hosted the Congress of 1878, where Bismarck called himself an 'honest broker' among the powers.", { w: 1 }),
], { w: 1, check: true, eight: E("Keep a new, central and exposed German state safe from a coalition of its neighbours.", "An emperor who could dismiss him; a Reichstag controlling the budget; France's wish to reverse 1871; Austria and Russia's rivalry in the Balkans.", "Preventive war on France; ally with Russia; ally with Austria; a web of balancing treaties.", "He annexed Alsace-Lorraine, then sought no more territory; tied Austria and Italy by treaty; kept a line to Russia.", "The emperor's trust; the Reichstag; the Prussian army; the 1878 Berlin Congress; treaties.", "Frederick III's death in 1888 and a young Wilhelm II who would not follow him.", "Peace among the great powers until 1914, but the system fell apart soon after his fall in 1890.", "Whether the web was wise or made war likelier; whether it could last without him; Gall's 'white revolutionary'.") });

const LEE = L("s9j", "Lee Kuan Yew", [98, -8, 112, 8], ["singapore", "kuala-lumpur", "malacca", "jakarta"], [
  S("Singapore left Malaysia on 9 August 1965, two years after joining it. It was a port city of about two million, with no hinterland, no oil, and water piped from Johor. Lee Kuan Yew (1923 to 2015) was prime minister from 1959 to 1990.", { ...X }),
  S("The government kept the British base for jobs until London announced its withdrawal in 1967. It set up the Economic Development Board (1961) to bring in foreign factories and built public housing on a vast scale. National service began in 1967. English became the language of work.", { ...X }),
  Q("Which was a constraint on Lee in 1965?", "A city-state with no land, no raw materials and neighbours who might act", ["A treaty limiting the size of its harbour", "An army that had to be paid from loans of the Soviet Union", "A parliament that could remove him by a no-confidence vote"], "The state had to trade its way out of small size. Water from Johor, defence and market access all depended on neighbours.", { k: "constraint" }),
  Q("Which was luck, not policy?", "A world boom in trade and container shipping", ["The founding of the Economic Development Board", "The decision to build public housing", "The introduction of national service"], "The boom favoured a port on a main sea route, and Vietnam War-era spending helped. Policy used it, but did not create it.", { k: "luck", ...X }),
  Q("Which statement about Singapore's rise is defensible?", "Historians weigh policy, location and the boom, and dispute copying it", ["All agree it proves that authoritarian rule produces growth", "All agree that it grew only because of its place on the sea route", "All agree that no policy of the government mattered"], "A small city-state on a trade route is an unusual case. Scholars also stress the economics minister Goh Keng Swee's part, and the costs for opponents.", { k: "reading", ...X }),
  S("The government used the Internal Security Act, with detention without trial, against communist and other opponents, as in Operation Coldstore (February 1963), and later libel suits. Critics see a limit on free politics; supporters see the price of order. This lesson sets out both views without taking a side.", X),
  Q("Why did Singapore's government hold on to the British base until 1967?", "It provided a large share of jobs and income", ["The treaty of 1965 made the base permanent", "The base was needed to defend the island against Indonesia", "Malaysia had demanded that Britain stay"], "The announced British withdrawal then forced a faster build of Singapore's own forces and industries.", { ...X }),
  T("Tap Singapore, at the southern end of the Malacca Strait.", "singapore", ["kuala-lumpur", "malacca", "jakarta"], "Singapore lies on an island at the end of the strait, which gave the port its value.", {}),
], { check: true, eight: E("Make a tiny, newly expelled port state viable between two larger neighbours.", "No land or resources; water and defence tied to neighbours; British forces leaving; mixed Chinese, Malay and Indian population.", "Rejoin Malaysia; live off the base; stay a trading post; industrialise for export.", "Export industry for foreign firms, public housing, strict order, a tight civil service, a conscript army.", "The People's Action Party; the Economic Development Board; the Housing Board; the Internal Security Act.", "The 1960s boom in trade; the Vietnam War's spending; a main sea route at its door.", "A rich city-state with one party dominant for decades.", "How far policy, location or luck explains it; the costs to opponents; whether it can be copied.") });

const SAUD = L("s9k", "The House of Saud: Abd al-Aziz and the founding of the kingdom", [33, 15, 60, 32], ["riyadh", "mecca", "jeddah", "dammam"], [
  S("This lesson is about the founding of the kingdom in the 1900s to 1930s, as historians describe it. It takes no position on the present government. Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud (c. 1876 to 1953) retook Riyadh in January 1902 from the Rashidi rulers of Hail, and from there united much of Arabia.", { ...X }),
  S("Three tools carried him. Marriage alliances and subsidies tied tribes to him. The Ikhwan, tribal settlers drawn to a strict Wahhabi creed, gave him fighters. And a treaty with Britain in 1915 gave him recognition and money. He took Mecca in 1924 and Jeddah in 1925.", { ...X }),
  Q("Which was a constraint on Abd al-Aziz in the 1920s?", "The Ikhwan, whose raids on neighbours risked war with Britain", ["A parliament elected by all the tribes of the Najd", "A treaty with the Ottoman sultan still in force then", "A British army of occupation camped in Riyadh"], "He needed the Ikhwan to win, but their raids into Iraq, Kuwait and Transjordan brought British air strikes. He crushed their revolt at Sabilla in March 1929.", { k: "constraint", ...X }),
  Q("Which was luck, not Abd al-Aziz's design?", "A commercial oil strike at Dammam in 1938", ["The grant of the oil concession in 1933", "The treaty with Britain in 1915", "The conquest of the Hejaz in 1924 and 1925"], "He chose to grant the concession in 1933, when pilgrim revenue had fallen in the Depression. The strike at Dammam in 1938 was fortune.", { k: "luck", ...X }),
  Q("Which statement about how the kingdom was founded is defensible?", "Historians weigh his skill, British money and religion differently", ["All agree it was mainly the work of British political officers", "All agree it needed no outside money or support at all", "All agree the religious alliance was of no importance"], "Scholars such as Kostiner and Al-Rasheed give different weight to personal rule, outside support and the Wahhabi alliance with the house of Saud, dating from about 1744.", { k: "reading", ...X }),
  O("Put these in order, earliest first.", ["Abd al-Aziz retakes Riyadh", "The treaty with Britain", "Mecca is taken", "The kingdom is named Saudi Arabia"], "1902, 1915, 1924 and 1932.", X),
  Q("What did Abd al-Aziz gain from the Hajj in the 1920s?", "Pilgrim fees and trade", ["A tax on British oil shipments", "Tribute from Egypt", "Arms from Ottoman depots"], "The Hejaz held Mecca and Medina. Revenue from pilgrims, which fell in the 1930s, was a prize and a reason for the oil concession.", X),
  T("Tap Riyadh, the town Abd al-Aziz retook in January 1902.", "riyadh", ["mecca", "jeddah", "dammam"], "Riyadh, in the Najd, was the Saud family's old seat. The oil fields lie near Dammam on the Gulf coast.", X),
], { check: true, eight: E("Unite a poor, tribal Arabia under one house with no resources but men and religion.", "Tribal rivals; the Ikhwan's demands; British power in the Gulf; Hashemite rivals; poverty before oil.", "Stay a Najdi chief; ally with Britain; use the Ikhwan; seek the Hejaz; seek foreign capital.", "He used marriage, subsidy and the Ikhwan, then crushed the Ikhwan; took the Hejaz; signed with Britain and Standard Oil of California.", "Tribal ties; the religious scholars; the family council; British treaties; the oil concession.", "The discovery of oil in 1938, after the concession of 1933.", "Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, proclaimed 23 September 1932; succession among his sons.", "How far it was his skill, British money or the religious alliance; the Ikhwan revolt; the role of oil.") });

const GANDHI = L("s9l", "Gandhi", [65, -30, 85, 30], ["porbandar", "dandi", "ahmedabad", "delhi"], [
  S("Mohandas Gandhi (1869 to 1948) was a lawyer who spent 1893 to 1914 in South Africa. There he developed satyagraha, firmness in truth: non-violent resistance. He returned to India in 1915 and by 1920 led the Indian National Congress in a mass movement.", { ...X }),
  S("His method was to refuse to cooperate and to accept the penalty. The Salt March of March and April 1930 walked from his ashram at Sabarmati to the coast at Dandi to break the salt law. Tens of thousands were jailed. The British could not tax what millions would not obey.", { ...X }),
  Q("Which was a constraint on Gandhi?", "A movement that could break into violence he could not control", ["A parliament of India that could vote him out", "A British veto on every Congress meeting", "A treaty requiring Congress to join the war"], "He called off the non-cooperation campaign in February 1922 after a mob killed policemen at Chauri Chaura. Discipline was his weak point and his method.", { k: "constraint", ...X }),
  Q("Which was luck, not Gandhi's strategy?", "Britain's exhaustion after the Second World War", ["The decision to march to Dandi", "The call to non-cooperation in 1920", "The Quit India resolution of 1942"], "Britain's finances and will were spent by 1945. Congress's campaigns raised the cost of rule, but the timing was not his.", { k: "luck", ...X }),
  Q("Which statement about why India became independent in 1947 is defensible?", "Historians weigh mass movements, war exhaustion and other pressures", ["All agree non-violent resistance alone caused it", "All agree it came only from Britain's own weakness at home", "All agree no Indian movement played any real part"], "Scholars weigh non-violence, the Indian National Army and the 1946 naval mutiny, labour unrest, and Britain's finances. The question is open.", { k: "reading", ...X }),
  S("The movement was a coalition and he could not hold it together. The Muslim League under Jinnah asked for separate safeguards and then Pakistan. Dr Ambedkar sought separate electorates for the depressed classes, which led to the Poona Pact of 1932. Partition came in 1947 with great violence.", X),
  Q("What decision of 1922 shows Gandhi's rule on violence?", "He called off the campaign after the Chauri Chaura killings", ["He ordered the Congress to take up arms in self-defence", "He accepted the British offer of dominion status", "He moved the Congress headquarters to London for a year"], "Many in Congress were angry. He held that a violent movement would be crushed and would betray its method.", X),
  T("Tap Dandi, the coastal village where the Salt March ended in April 1930.", "dandi", ["porbandar", "ahmedabad", "delhi"], "Dandi lies on the Gujarat coast. The march began at Sabarmati near Ahmedabad.", {}),
], { check: true, eight: E("Win self-rule for a vast, divided country from an empire that held the army, the courts and the money.", "A coalition of Hindus, Muslims and others; British repression; the need for non-violent discipline; the war.", "Petition within the law; armed revolt; terror; mass non-violent non-cooperation.", "Mass civil disobedience in waves, called off when violence came; talks when the British offered them.", "The Congress as a mass party; the press; provincial government from 1937; the British Raj.", "The Second World War and Britain's weakness, which came after the campaigns.", "Independence and partition in August 1947; Gandhi assassinated in January 1948.", "How much non-violence caused independence; who is to blame for partition; his views and his record in South Africa.") });

const DENG = L("s9m", "Deng Xiaoping", [100, 20, 125, 42], ["khanbaliq", "shenzhen", "shanghai", "guangzhou"], [
  S("Deng Xiaoping (1904 to 1997) joined the Communist Party in France in the 1920s. He was purged twice, in 1966 and again in 1976, and came back for good after Mao died in September 1976. In December 1978 the Party's third plenum shifted its work to the economy.", X),
  S("The reforms began in the countryside, where villages tried household farming, and in 1980 four special economic zones, Shenzhen next to Hong Kong the best known, were opened to foreign firms. Deng's rule of thumb was to cross the river by feeling the stones, a saying often credited to Chen Yun.", X),
  Q("Which did Deng have to reckon with in 1978?", "Party elders who feared that markets would undo socialism", ["A parliamentary opposition able to block reform votes", "Soviet occupation troops in Beijing", "A loan agreement forbidding market prices"], "He worked inside a one-party system; the check on him was the Party's elders and its old guard, not a legislature.", { k: "constraint" }),
  Q("Which was luck, not policy choice?", "Mao's death in 1976, which let Deng return", ["The decision to open special economic zones in 1980", "The talks with the United States for ties in 1979", "The third plenum's decision to shift the focus to the economy"], "Mao's death and the arrest of the Gang of Four opened the way for Deng's return. The other three were decisions.", { k: "luck" }),
  Q("Which statement about China's growth after 1978 is defensible?", "Historians weigh planning, local trials and outside capital", ["All agree it was designed entirely by Deng from the start", "All agree it happened with no role for the Party", "All agree it was caused only by foreign aid and loans"], "Many reforms began locally, and some were legalised after the fact. Scholars also weigh cheap labour, a young population and investment from Hong Kong and Taiwan.", { k: "reading", ...X }),
  Q("What happened in June 1989 and after, and how does it figure in the case?", "Troops cleared the protests; Deng restarted reform on a southern tour", ["Deng resigned and the reforms were abandoned for good", "A referendum approved the new zones in the south", "The army split and a new party took power"], "The crackdown of 3 and 4 June 1989 slowed reform. The 1992 tour restarted it. Historians dispute Deng's part in the decision, and the toll is argued.", { k: "reading", ...X }),
  O("Put these in order, earliest first.", ["Mao dies", "The third plenum shifts the focus to the economy", "Shenzhen is made a special economic zone", "The southern tour"], "1976, 1978, 1980 and 1992.", X),
  T("Tap Shenzhen, the first and best known special economic zone, set up in 1980.", "shenzhen", ["shanghai", "khanbaliq", "guangzhou"], "Shenzhen, beside Hong Kong, was one of four zones created in 1980, where markets and foreign investment were tried first.", {}),
], { check: true, eight: E("Raise a poor, damaged economy after Mao without losing the Party's hold or its legitimacy.", "Party elders and the planned economy; Mao's legacy; little foreign exchange or know-how; Taiwan and Hong Kong.", "Stay with planning; copy the Soviet reforms; open the coast to markets and foreign firms.", "Rural household farming, special economic zones, ties with the United States and Japan, a younger leadership.", "The Party; the special economic zones; the retirement of elders; the central bank and ministries.", "Mao's death in 1976; Hong Kong and Taiwanese capital; a young workforce.", "Decades of growth under continued one-party rule.", "How far Deng planned it; the part of local experiments; 1989; the cost of the model.") });

